tle": "The Daniell Cell", "question": "A zinc strip stands in zinc sulfate, a copper strip in copper sulfate, joined by a wire through a voltmeter and by a salt bridge. The meter reads 1.10 V; over hours the zinc thins and the copper strip thickens. Which electrode gives up electrons, which way do they flow, and what sets the voltage?", "model_answer": "Zn is the anode (E° = −0.7618 V, oxidized: Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻); electrons flow through the wire to Cu, the cathode (Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu, E° = +0.337 V). Anions in the salt bridge drift toward the zinc. E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode = 0.337 − (−0.7618) = 1.099 V.", "target": "In a galvanic cell the half-reaction with the lower E° runs as oxidation at the anode; E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode.", "keywords": ["anode", "cathode", "oxidation", "reduction", "electrons flow", "wire", "salt bridge", "anions", "E° cathode minus E° anode", "zinc", "copper"], "liquids": ["DN1", "DN2", "DN3", "DN4", "DN5", "DN6"], "kinds": ["cell"], "fits": ["DN1", "DN2", "DN3", "DN4", "DN5", "DN6"], "media": {"image_with_question": "ch17/Daniell_cell.jpg", "image_after_account": "", "image_alt": "", "simulation": "ch17/cell_simulator.html?left={left}&right={right}&theme={theme}", "simulation_opens": "meaning", "credit": "AI image made by the instructor with Gemini"}, "label_words": ["galvanic cell", "anode", "cathode", "salt bridge", "standard cell potential"], "role": "worked", "items": {"add": [], "remove": []}}, {"title": "The Science Fair Battery", "question": "A student builds cells from pairs of metals, each strip in a 1 M solution of its own ion, and records each pair's voltage. Magnesium with copper gives the highest reading. Without measuring, how can you tell which metal of a pair is the anode and what the voltage will be?", "model_answer": "Look up both reduction potentials in Appendix L: the metal with the lower E° is oxidized (anode). E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode. Mg/Cu: 0.337 − (−2.372) = 2.709 V, the largest gap among the pairs.", "target": "The anode is the half-reaction with the lower reduction potential; the voltage is the difference between the two.", "keywords": ["lower E°", "anode", "oxidized", "cathode", "difference", "E° cathode minus E° anode", "Appendix L", "reduction potential"], "liquids": ["SF1", "SF2", "SF3", "SF4", "SF5", "SF6"], "kinds": ["cell"], "fits": ["SF1", "SF2", "SF3", "SF4", "SF5", "SF6"], "media": {"image_with_question": "ch17/Science_fair_battery.jpg", "image_after_account": "", "image_alt": "", "simulation": "ch17/cell_simulator.html?left={left}&right={right}&theme={theme}", "simulation_opens": "meaning", "credit": "AI image made by the instructor with Gemini"}, "label_words": ["standard reduction potential", "galvanic cell", "anode", "cathode"], "role": "exam", "items": {"add": [], "remove": []}}, {"title": "The Statue of Liberty", "question": "The Statue of Liberty's copper skin was riveted to an iron frame with only insulating pads between them. Where moisture bridged the two metals, the iron frame corroded badly, and restorers in the 1980s replaced it. Which metal is oxidized when copper and iron touch in salty water, and why that one?", "model_answer": "Fe²⁺/Fe, E° = −0.447 V; Cu²⁺/Cu, +0.337 V. Iron has the lower potential, so it is oxidized (anode): Fe + Cu²⁺ → Fe²⁺ + Cu, E°cell = 0.784 V > 0, spontaneous. The reverse, copper with Fe²⁺, has E°cell = −0.784 V: no reaction.", "target": "Of two metals in contact, the one with the lower reduction potential is oxidized; a positive E°cell means the reaction runs on its own.", "keywords": ["iron", "oxidized", "lower E°", "anode", "corrodes", "copper", "positive E°cell", "spontaneous", "galvanic corrosion"], "liquids": ["SL1", "SL2", "SL3", "SL4", "SL5", "SL6"], "kinds": ["reaction"], "fits": ["SL1", "SL2", "SL3", "SL4", "SL5", "SL6"], "media": {"image_with_question": "ch17/Statue_of_liberty.jpg", "image_after_account": "", "image_alt": "", "simulation": "ch17/cell_simulator.html?left={left}&right={right}&theme={theme}", "s